<section class="page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">ADDRESSING / SERVICE DESIGN</span><h1>Shared where it fits.<br><em>Dedicated where it matters.</em></h1><p class="lede">CGNAT, direct IPv4 and static IPv4 are different service choices. Plan them alongside IPv6, subscriber identity and operational evidence.</p></section><section class="section"><div class="address-flow" aria-label="Addressing architecture"><span>Subscriber identity<br><b>RADIUS & service intent</b></span><span>Addressing policy<br><b>CGNAT / direct / static</b></span><span>Internet edge<br><b>Routing + IPv6 context</b></span></div><p class="fine">Architectural illustration. The browser demo uses reserved addresses and simulated allocation; it does not translate packets.</p></section><section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">THREE IPV4 MODES</span><h2>A policy choice per service.</h2><div class="cards"><article><h3>CGNAT / shared IPv4</h3><p>A subscriber uses private IPv4 behind a shared public address. Capacity depends on public resources, ports, state, traffic patterns and logging. A subscriber count alone is not a sizing result.</p></article><article><h3>Direct / no translation</h3><p>Route subscriber IPv4 without CGNAT. Plan the usable prefix, upstream return route and service policy. Direct addressing does not by itself promise a permanent address.</p></article><article><h3>Static / dedicated assignment</h3><p>Reserve an explicit address for a service that needs a stable endpoint. Validate uniqueness and routing, then keep the assignment attached across normal policy changes.</p></article></div></section><section class="split section"><div><span class="eyebrow">A CHANGE YOU CAN FOLLOW</span><h2>From CGNAT<br>to a static address.</h2><p>Search the demo by username, IP, IPv6, name or MAC, then open the subscriber’s addressing controls. Explore the complete decision instead of a checkbox.</p><a class="button" href="https://demo.liquidnat.com/">Try the addressing workflow ↗</a></div><div class="stack"><article><h3>1. Review the assignment</h3><p>Choose static IPv4 and enter a reserved demo address. Validation rejects duplicates and invalid values.</p></article><article><h3>2. Review the impact</h3><p>See the transition before applying. The simulated allocation releases CGNAT capacity and ends the old mapping interval.</p></article><article><h3>3. Check the result</h3><p>The service becomes a NAT bypass assignment. IPv6 context and historical mapping intervals remain available.</p></article></div></section><section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">TRACEABILITY & IPV6</span><h2>Keep the complete service in view.</h2><div class="cards"><article><h3>A lookup needs time</h3><p>External IPv4, source port, protocol and timestamp identify the translation event. Current ownership alone cannot explain a historical connection.</p></article><article><h3>Separate failure types</h3><p>Public-pool exhaustion, port-block exhaustion and state pressure have different causes. Test admission and existing sessions independently.</p></article><article><h3>IPv6 is its own path</h3><p>WAN addressing and delegated prefixes are separate facts. Validate return routes, MTU and policy in both directions.</p></article><article><h3>Retention is a design input</h3><p>Agree mapping evidence, access restrictions, storage sizing, export and deletion with your operations team. Synthetic demo history is not a compliance service.</p></article></div></section>
